# Diagnose dispersion in fishing-count data

This lesson is a bounded one-to-one port of the opening fishing case study in [Chapter 13 of *Statistics in Natural Resources: Applications with R*](https://stats4nr.com/count-regression-models). It uses the exact package data and the exact published histogram. It deliberately stops before regression because BioLang's current Poisson fit does not reproduce the source model.

## Load the exact source data

```biolang
let fishing = read_csv("data/fishing.csv")
{observations: nrow(fishing), variables: ncol(fishing), first_rows: head(fishing, 10)}
```

The six variables record whether a group caught no fish, used live bait, brought a camper, its total people and children, and its fish count.

## Exact published histogram

![Published histogram of fish caught](figures/source/fishing-histogram.png)

This is the PNG served by the source chapter, copied byte for byte. It is not a BioLang redraw. The plot shows many zero or low catches and a small number of much larger catches.

## Compare the mean and variance

```biolang
let catches = fishing |> col("count")
let catch_mean = mean(catches)
let catch_variance = variance(catches)
{
    mean_fish_caught: catch_mean,
    variance_fish_caught: catch_variance,
    variance_to_mean_ratio: catch_variance / catch_mean
}
```

The chapter reports a mean of 3.296 and a sample variance of 135.3739. Because the variance is far larger than the mean, the response is overdispersed relative to the Poisson mean-equals-variance assumption.

## Quantify the excess zeros

```biolang
let zero_catches = fishing |> filter(|row| row.count == 0)
let zero_percent = nrow(zero_catches) * 100.0 / nrow(fishing)
{groups_catching_zero_fish: nrow(zero_catches), percent_catching_zero_fish: zero_percent}
```

The source later reports that 56.8% of groups caught zero fish. That reinforces why the chapter compares overdispersed and zero-inflated count models.

## Fidelity checks

```biolang
assert nrow(fishing) == 250, "exact source observation count"
assert ncol(fishing) == 6, "exact source variable count"
assert abs(catch_mean - 3.296) < 0.000000001, "published mean"
assert abs(catch_variance - 135.373879518072) < 0.000000001, "published sample variance"
assert nrow(zero_catches) == 142, "exact zero-catch count"
assert abs(zero_percent - 56.8) < 0.000000001, "published zero-catch percentage"
"Exact source data and published-figure checks passed."
```
